[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering CSS3: The Cascade, Specificity, and Inheritance"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing CSS3, understanding how the browser resolves conflicting style rules is crucial for building predictable and maintainable layouts. The rendering engine relies on three core mechanisms: the Cascade, Specificity, and Inheritance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Cascade"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The cascade is the algorithm that defines how to solve styling conflicts. When multiple rules apply to the same element, the cascade determines which one wins based on three primary factors:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Origin and Importance: User agent, user, and author stylesheets interact, with 'important' declarations overriding normal ones."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Specificity: More specific selectors take precedence over less specific ones."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Source Order: If conflicting rules have the exact same specificity and origin, the rule that appears last in the stylesheet wins."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Specificity"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Specificity is a scoring system calculated by the browser to determine which CSS declaration is most relevant to an element. It is typically represented as a three-value metric: (ID, Class, Element)."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"/* Specificity: 0, 0, 1 */
p { color: blue; }

/* Specificity: 0, 1, 0 */
.text-highlight { color: green; }

/* Specificity: 1, 0, 0 */
#unique-header { color: red; }"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, an element matched by both the paragraph selector and the class selector will be green because the class has a higher specificity weight."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Inheritance"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is the mechanism by which certain properties are passed down from a parent element to its descendants. Properties related to typography, such as 'font-family', 'color', and 'line-height', are typically inherited."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"body {
  font-family: "Helvetica Neue", sans-serif;
  color: #333333;
}

/* The paragraph automatically inherits font-family and color */
p {
  margin: 0;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Box model properties like 'margin', 'padding', 'border', and 'width' are never inherited by default. You can explicitly force inheritance using the 'inherit' keyword if needed."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Clean CSS"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using IDs for styling to keep your specificity scores low and manageable."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rely on class-based selectors and leverage modern methodologies like BEM (Block Element Modifier)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the browser's developer tools to inspect computed styles and debug unexpected overrides."}]
